1
00:00:00,261 --> 00:00:02,253
How do you find
an extinct giant

2
00:00:02,253 --> 00:00:03,872
without finding its body?

3
00:00:04,324 --> 00:00:05,524
In northern Greenland,

4
00:00:05,551 --> 00:00:08,248
scientists found traces
of mastodons

5
00:00:08,387 --> 00:00:10,292
in sediment about
two million years old.

6
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:12,093
The clue wasn’t a skeleton.

7
00:00:12,615 --> 00:00:14,312
It was broken DNA.

8
00:00:14,781 --> 00:00:17,270
Living things leave
genetic traces behind.

9
00:00:17,565 --> 00:00:19,575
Some fragments can
bind to minerals,

10
00:00:19,810 --> 00:00:20,880
helping them survive.

11
00:00:21,254 --> 00:00:23,403
Scientists compared
the damaged pieces

12
00:00:23,716 --> 00:00:24,795
with known DNA.

13
00:00:25,178 --> 00:00:26,666
Together with plant DNA,

14
00:00:26,779 --> 00:00:29,537
they revealed evidence of
a vanished forest ecosystem.

15
00:00:30,032 --> 00:00:31,703
Not a resurrected animal.

16
00:00:31,929 --> 00:00:33,617
Not a complete genome.

17
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:35,366
But here’s the harder mystery:

18
00:00:35,644 --> 00:00:37,749
how do you know
those tiny pieces

19
00:00:38,062 --> 00:00:39,716
belong to a mastodon?

20
00:00:39,959 --> 00:00:41,603
Follow the full investigation

21
00:00:41,882 --> 00:00:42,839
in the linked video.
